I fancy I see you ''bless yourself'' at this terrible relation. {{right|''Lady M. W. Montagu'', Letters, II. 47. (''N. E. D.'')}}}}
'''4†.''' To defend; preserve; protect or guard from evil; reflexively, to guard one’s self from; avoid; eschew.
{{smaller block|<poem>And, were not hevenly grace that did him ''blesse'',
He had beene pouldred all, as thin as flowre.</poem>{{right|''Spenser'', F. Q., I. vii. 12.}}
<poem>''Bless'' me from this woman! I would stand the cannon,
Before ten words of hers.</poem>{{right|''Fletcher'', Wildgoose Chase, i. 3.}}

And therefore God ''bless'' us from that [separation by death], and I will hope well of the rest. {{right|''Arabella Stuart'', in D’Israeli’s Curios. of Lit., II. 277.}}}}
'''5.''' To invoke or pronounce a blessing upon (another or others); commend to God’s favor or protection.
{{smaller block|And Isaac called Jacob, and ''blessed'' him. {{right|Gen. xxviii. 1.}}
<poem>A thousand times I ''blest'' him, as he knelt beside my bed.</poem>{{right|''Tennyson'', May Queen.}}}}
'''6.''' To confer well-being upon; bestow happiness, prosperity, or good of any kind upon; make happy, prosperous, or fortunate; prosper with temporal or spiritual benefits: as, a nation ''blessed'' with peace and plenty.
{{smaller block|The Lord thy God shall ''bless'' thee in all that thou doest. {{right|Deut. xv. 18.}}

Heaven ''bless'' your expedition. {{right|''Shak.'', 2 Hen. IV., i. 2.}}}}
